::  RandomGenerator — java.util.Random-exact PRNG (unit 9.1)
::
::  Ports tlc2.util.RandomGenerator (which extends java.util.Random) so seeded
::  simulation traces match TLC byte for byte. The core is java.util.Random's
::  48-bit linear congruential generator; RandomGenerator adds nextPrime and
::  the seed(seed,cnt) fast-forward. State is threaded explicitly as the raw
::  48-bit LCG value `s`; each draw gate returns [value new-s]. Output atoms are
::  two's complement: next-int is 32-bit, next-long 64-bit. next-double is a
::  non-negative @rd in [0,1) (its atom is the IEEE-754 double, so its raw bits
::  equal Java's Double.doubleToRawLongBits). floor-scaled reproduces the
::  Math.floor(nextDouble()*n) index used by SimulationWorker for state/action
::  choice; the same IEEE-754 multiply/floor drives nextPrime.
::
|%
::  +|  %constants
::
++  a-mul  0x5.deec.e66d       ::  java.util.Random multiplier
++  a-add  0xb                 ::  java.util.Random addend
++  a-bits  48                 ::  LCG modulus is 2^48
++  d-bits  53                 ::  double mantissa width (nextDouble scale)
::
::  +|  %core
::
++  scramble
  ::  java.util.Random seed scrambling: (seed ^ multiplier) & (2^48 - 1). The
  ::  high bits of a 64-bit Java seed fall outside the mask, so it takes @.
  |=  seed=@
  ^-  @
  (dis (mix seed a-mul) (dec (bex a-bits)))
::
++  nx
  ::  java.util.Random.next(bits): advance the LCG one step and return the top
  ::  `bits` bits. Returns [value new-state]; value is an unsigned `bits`-wide
  ::  atom (for bits=32 this is the two's-complement image of a Java int).
  |=  [s=@ bits=@]
  ^-  [@ @]
  =/  ns  (mod (add (mul s a-mul) a-add) (bex a-bits))
  [(rsh [0 (sub a-bits bits)] ns) ns]
::
++  next-int
  ::  java.util.Random.nextInt(): a 32-bit two's-complement draw
  |=  s=@
  ^-  [@ @]
  (nx s 32)
::
++  next-long
  ::  java.util.Random.nextLong(): ((long)next(32) << 32) + next(32), computed
  ::  in two's complement mod 2^64; the low draw is sign-extended first.
  |=  s=@
  ^-  [@ @]
  =^  hi  s  (nx s 32)
  =^  lo  s  (nx s 32)
  =/  slo  ?:((lth lo (bex 31)) lo (add lo 0xffff.ffff.0000.0000))
  [(mod (add (lsh [0 32] hi) slo) (bex 64)) s]
::
++  next-double
  ::  java.util.Random.nextDouble(): ((next(26) << 27) + next(27)) * 2^-53.
  ::  The 53-bit numerator is < 2^53, so m/2^53 is an exact IEEE-754 double.
  |=  s=@
  ^-  [@rd @]
  =^  a  s  (nx s 26)
  =^  b  s  (nx s 27)
  =/  m  (add (lsh [0 27] a) b)
  [(div:rd (sun:rd m) (sun:rd (bex d-bits))) s]
::
++  floor-scaled
  ::  Math.floor(nextDouble() * n): the seeded index into an n-element sequence,
  ::  matching SimulationWorker.randomState and getNextActionIndex. The multiply
  ::  is a single IEEE-754 rounding; the floor truncates toward zero (n, the
  ::  product, and the double are all non-negative).
  |=  [s=@ n=@]
  ^-  [@ @]
  =^  d  s  (next-double s)
  [(abs:si (need (toi:rd (mul:rd d (sun:rd n))))) s]
::
++  next-prime
  ::  RandomGenerator.nextPrime(): primes[floor(nextDouble() * primes-len)].
  ::  The Java loop retries while the index equals the length; since nextDouble
  ::  is < 1 the index is always < length, so exactly one draw is taken.
  |=  s=@
  ^-  [@ @]
  =/  len  (lent primes)
  |-  ^-  [@ @]
  =^  i  s  (floor-scaled s len)
  ?:  =(i len)  $(s s)
  [(snag i primes) s]
::
++  seed-cnt
  ::  RandomGenerator.setSeed(seed, cnt): scramble, then advance by `cnt`
  ::  nextDouble() draws (each consuming two LCG steps). Returns the state, from
  ::  which the next draw resumes (the persisted equivalent of getAril).
  |=  [seed=@ cnt=@]
  ^-  @
  =/  s  (scramble seed)
  |-  ^-  @
  ?:  =(cnt 0)  s
  =^  d  s  (next-double s)
  $(cnt (dec cnt))
::
::  +|  %primes
::
++  primes
  ::  RandomGenerator.primes: 1.341 fixed primes near 1.28e6 (verbatim from the
  ::  Java source array); nextPrime indexes this table.
  ^-  (list @ud)
